---
import "../../../../packages/ui/src/brand/brand.css";
import "../../../../packages/ui/src/glass/glass.css";
import "../installer.css";

import Footer from "../../../../packages/ui/src/brand/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../../../packages/ui/src/brand/Header.astro";
import ThemeInit from '../../../../packages/ui/src/brand/ThemeInit.astro';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head><ThemeInit external />
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Run the installer in your account · SAM</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="/sam-head.png" />
    
    
    
    <script is:inline src="/guide.js" defer></script>
  </head>
  <body class="sam-form">
    <Header compact icon="/brand-icon.png" />
    <main class="privacy-main">

      <span class="badge">Your account. Your installer.</span>
      <h1>Run the installer<br /><span class="gradient-text">in your account.</span></h1>
      <p class="intro">
        One command puts this open-source installer in your own Cloudflare account. No GitHub
        account is needed to get SAM running.
      </p>
      <div class="privacy-content sam-glass sam-glass-rim">
        <h2>1. Create a Cloudflare API token</h2>
        <p>
          The token needs three account permissions: Workers Scripts Edit, Account Settings Read
          and OAuth Client Edit. Limit it to the account you are installing into and give it a
          short lifetime. It only deploys the installer; the permissions SAM needs are approved
          later, when you sign in with Cloudflare.
        </p>
        <h2>2. Get the source</h2>
        <p>
          You’ll need Git, Node.js 22.23.4+ or 24+, pnpm 9.15.9, a Workers Paid account, and an
          active domain in Cloudflare for SAM itself.
          <a id="source" href="https://github.com/raphaeltm/simple-agent-manager"
            >Inspect the source →</a
          >
        </p>
        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/raphaeltm/simple-agent-manager.git
cd simple-agent-manager
<span id="revision-command">git checkout "$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0)"
</span>pnpm install --frozen-lockfile</code></pre>
        <p>
          The installer installs exactly the version you check out, so it must be a commit that
          exists in the SAM repository.
        </p>
        <h2>3. Deploy</h2>
        <p>
          Set <code>CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN</code> and <code>CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID</code> through your
          shell’s secret manager. Never commit credentials or paste them into chat.
        </p>
        <pre><code>pnpm --filter @simple-agent-manager/installer run deploy</code></pre>
        <p>
          The command prints your installer’s address on your account’s
          <code>workers.dev</code>. It also registers a private OAuth client, which only members of
          your Cloudflare account can authorize, and saves its credentials as Worker secrets.
          Running it again keeps both. Set <code>BASE_DOMAIN=example.com</code> first to serve the
          installer at <code>install.example.com</code> instead; the token then also needs that
          domain’s Workers Routes and Custom Domains permissions.
        </p>
        <h2>4. Install, then remove the installer</h2>
        <p>
          Open the address, sign in with Cloudflare and follow the steps. Once your installation
          service is yours, the installer has done its job: updates come from the installation
          service.
        </p>
        <pre><code>pnpm --filter @simple-agent-manager/installer run remove</code></pre>
        <p>
          Removing the installer never touches your SAM or its installation service. A full
          installation is still labelled experimental.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/privacy.html">Review permissions & privacy →</a></p>
      </div>
    </main>
    <Footer compact icon="/sam-head.png"><a href="/privacy.html">Permissions & privacy</a><a href="/self-host.html">Self-host</a><span id="version"></span></Footer>
  </body>
</html>
